.TH PTYSH 1
.SH NAME
ptysh \- run a command in a separate terminal session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ptysh
.RI [ command " [argument ...]]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Ptysh allocates an unused pseudo-terminal pair and starts a new session with
its slave as the controlling terminal. The default command is sh. Input,
output, terminal signals, line editing, editors and shell job control travel
through the PTY. Exit the inner shell to return to the outer shell.
.PP
FUZIX/Ripple has eight PTY pairs: /dev/ptyp0 through /dev/ptyp7
and /dev/ttyp0 through /dev/ttyp7. Masters open exclusively, with mode
0600 on installed device nodes. /dev/tty resolves to the caller's controlling
terminal. Closing the master hangs up the slave's foreground process group;
closing the last slave allows the master to drain output and then read EOF.
.PP
Ptysh requires an interactive outer terminal. It preserves terminal settings
and restores them on exit. Nested ptysh sessions allocate different pairs.
The fixed pool, process slots and per-process descriptors limit nesting.
.PP
This bridge uses nonblocking I/O and the existing terminal timeout mechanism.
It is a minimal PTY host, not a terminal multiplexer or graphical terminal.
.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), tty(1), stty(1), ps(1)
